    </table>
  </div>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Pinterest recommends a 2:3 aspect ratio, or 1000 by 1500 pixels, for a standard image pin, as a PNG or JPEG of up to 20 MB from a desktop or 32 MB in the app, and warns that "Pins with an aspect ratio greater than 2:3 might get cut off in people's feeds." Read directly from the specs page, which carries no date.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Pinterest Help Center, "Review ad specs," read October 15, 2026. https://help.pinterest.com/en/business/article/pinterest-product-specs</p>

  <p>The specs page sits in Pinterest's business help, but it isn't only for ads. Each format on it says whether it can run in paid campaigns, organic impressions or both, and the help article on creating a pin sends readers to it for "technical details."</p>

  <p>Two lines on the specs page trip people up. The first is color: Pinterest turns every upload into an 8-bit RGB JPEG and doesn't support custom color profiles, so colors can shift. It asks for PNG in sRGB to avoid losing quality.</p>

  <p>The second is a tint. On a white or black background, Pinterest lays a faint layer over image pins in the home feed: black at 4 percent over a near-white pin in light mode, white at 10 percent over a near-black pin in dark mode. It does this to every image pin, organic or paid, so in light mode a white-background packshot never shows pure white in the feed. Video is exempt.</p>

  <p>On the description, Pinterest's pages split. The Help Center says 800 characters; the Business best-practices page says 500. Write to 500 and both are satisfied. Neither shows the description in the home or search feed anyway, though the Help Center says Pinterest reads it to decide who sees the pin.</p>

  <h2>What happens to a Pinterest pin taller than 2:3?</h2>

  <p>It might be cut off. That's Pinterest's whole answer: "Pins with an aspect ratio greater than 2:3 might get cut off in people's feeds," on the specs page, and "Larger aspect ratios may get cut off in people's feeds" on the Business page.</p>

  <p>Neither page gives a height where the cut starts, a pixel size for a tall pin, or a minimum size. The 1000 by 2100 "long pin," the 1000 by 3000 "infographic pin" and the 600 by 900 minimum that size guides repeat aren't on any Pinterest page read for this guide, and the guides that print them cite none.</p>

  <p>What works in production instead (our practice, not Pinterest's rule): build the pin at 2:3, keep the headline and the product in the top two thirds, and if a long graphic really needs the room, split it into a carousel.</p>

